@meta
id: 29-07
title: Taking a pair away
chapter: 29
type: lesson
skills: surround
par: 20
minutes: 4
vscode: two clicks and two backspaces, five times
open: selectors.ts
vimrc: set packpath^=. | packloadall!

@brief
The surround plugin is shipped with this lesson, in
`pack/dojo/start/surround/plugin/surround.vim`. Third and last of its
verbs: *delete surround*. Take the pair off, leave what is inside exactly
where it was.

Prettier has been reconfigured with `arrowParens: "avoid"`, so a
single-argument arrow function is written without brackets around the
argument. `selectors.ts` has five of them, all still bracketed. Strip the
brackets.

Deleting two characters that are twenty columns apart is exactly the kind
of job that feels too small to think about and then costs you four moves
and a mistake.

@keys
| `ds{char}` | delete the pair of `{char}` surrounding the cursor |
| `ds(` | drop the brackets, keep what is inside |
| `ds"` `ds'` | drop the quotes |
| `f(` | you still have to be inside the pair — brackets do not search |

@hints
`ds` and then the character of the pair you want gone.
--
Unlike the quote objects, a bracket object will not go looking down the
line for you: the cursor has to be on the bracket or inside it. Get there
with `f(` first.
--
`f(` `ds(` on the first line, recorded as a macro with `j0` on the end,
then replayed four times.

@solution
    qq f( ds( j0 q  4@q  :wq<Enter>

Seventeen keystrokes, five functions.

The `f(` is the interesting half. Quote text objects search forward along
the line, so `cs'"` worked from column zero last lesson. Bracket objects
do not — `i(` means "inside the brackets I am in", and if you are in none,
nothing happens. That asymmetry is vim's, not the plugin's, and it catches
everyone once.

Read the plugin now that you have used all three verbs. `ds`, `cs` and
`ys` are one short function each and one mapping each, and `ds` reads in
full: yank what is inside the pair, then select the pair and everything
in it and paste the inside back over the selection. Every plugin you will ever
install is that — vimscript standing on the same commands you type by
hand. Nothing in a plugin is available to the plugin and not to you.

@start pack/dojo/start/surround/plugin/surround.vim
" dojo-surround.vim - a small, honest surround plugin.
" Shipped inside this lesson. You did not install it; the dojo did.
"
"   ds{char}           delete the surrounding pair
"   cs{old}{new}       change one surrounding pair into another
"   ys{motion}{char}   surround the text the motion covers
"   {Visual}S{char}    surround the selection
"
" Brackets come in pairs: ( [ { < all give you the matching closer.
" Anything else is used on both sides, so " gives "..." and ` gives `...`.

if exists('g:loaded_dojo_surround')
  finish
endif
let g:loaded_dojo_surround = 1

let s:pairs = {
      \ '(': '()', ')': '()',
      \ '[': '[]', ']': '[]',
      \ '{': '{}', '}': '{}',
      \ '<': '<>', '>': '<>',
      \ }

function! s:delims(char) abort
  return get(s:pairs, a:char, a:char . a:char)
endfunction

" The text between the pair, without the pair itself.
function! s:inner(char) abort
  let save = @z
  let @z = ''
  silent execute 'normal! "zyi' . a:char
  let text = @z
  let @z = save
  return text
endfunction

" A visual selection covering the pair AND what is between it. vi" selects
" the inside; o h o l then walks each end out by one character onto the
" delimiters. Doing it this way rather than with va" matters: va" also
" swallows the whitespace next to the string.
function! s:select(char) abort
  return 'vi' . a:char . 'ohol'
endfunction

function! s:replace(char, text) abort
  let save = @z
  let @z = a:text
  silent execute 'normal! ' . s:select(a:char) . '"zp'
  let @z = save
endfunction

function! s:ds(char) abort
  let inner = s:inner(a:char)
  if inner ==# ''
    return
  endif
  call s:replace(a:char, inner)
endfunction

function! s:cs(old, new) abort
  let inner = s:inner(a:old)
  if inner ==# ''
    return
  endif
  let d = s:delims(a:new)
  call s:replace(a:old, d[0] . inner . d[1])
endfunction

function! s:ys(type) abort
  let d = s:delims(nr2char(getchar()))
  let save = @z
  if a:type ==# 'line'
    silent execute "normal! '[V']\"zy"
    call setreg('z', d[0] . "\n" . @z . d[1], 'V')
    silent execute "normal! '[V']\"zp"
  else
    silent execute "normal! `[v`]\"zy"
    let @z = d[0] . @z . d[1]
    silent execute "normal! `[v`]\"zp"
  endif
  let @z = save
endfunction

function! s:vs(char) abort
  let d = s:delims(a:char)
  let save = @z
  silent execute 'normal! gv"zy'
  let @z = d[0] . @z . d[1]
  silent execute 'normal! gv"zp'
  let @z = save
endfunction

nnoremap <silent> ds :<C-u>call <SID>ds(nr2char(getchar()))<CR>
nnoremap <silent> cs :<C-u>call <SID>cs(nr2char(getchar()), nr2char(getchar()))<CR>
nnoremap <silent> ys :<C-u>set operatorfunc=<SID>ys<CR>g@
xnoremap <silent> S :<C-u>call <SID>vs(nr2char(getchar()))<CR>

@start selectors.ts
export const orderId = (order) => order.id;
export const orderTotal = (order) => order.total;
export const orderCustomer = (order) => order.customerId;
export const isCancelled = (order) => order.status === 'CANCELLED';
export const isRefundable = (payment) => payment.capturedAt !== null;

@expected selectors.ts
export const orderId = order => order.id;
export const orderTotal = order => order.total;
export const orderCustomer = order => order.customerId;
export const isCancelled = order => order.status === 'CANCELLED';
export const isRefundable = payment => payment.capturedAt !== null;
